WEBVTT

00:00:06.735 --> 00:00:09.960
We will save a score card, restore it from our own

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:13.120
temporary file, and compare its saved values with

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:14.152
temporary state.

00:00:14.152 --> 00:00:17.248
Watch how the program checks the restored object

00:00:17.248 --> 00:00:18.860
before using its methods.

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:10.580
Object serialization writes an object state

00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:12.746
representation into a byte stream.

00:01:12.746 --> 00:01:15.868
Serializable is a marker interface: it marks this

00:01:15.868 --> 00:01:18.034
support without requiring methods.

00:01:18.034 --> 00:01:21.475
Objects held in saved reference fields must support it

00:01:21.475 --> 00:01:21.730
too.

00:01:21.730 --> 00:01:24.788
The serialization version identifier helps check

00:01:24.788 --> 00:01:27.910
whether saved data and the current class can work

00:01:27.910 --> 00:01:28.484
together.

00:01:28.484 --> 00:01:31.733
Its long type stores whole numbers, and the L marks

00:01:31.733 --> 00:01:33.453
the written number as long.

00:01:33.453 --> 00:01:36.639
Matching identifiers alone do not make every class

00:01:36.639 --> 00:01:37.786
change compatible.

00:01:37.786 --> 00:01:41.227
Static fields belong to the class and are not saved as

00:01:41.227 --> 00:01:42.182
instance state.

00:01:42.182 --> 00:01:45.432
A transient instance field is excluded from default

00:01:45.432 --> 00:01:46.324
serialization.

00:01:46.324 --> 00:01:48.490
Here, views marks temporary state.

00:01:48.804 --> 00:01:52.068
Object deserialization reconstructs an object from a

00:01:52.068 --> 00:01:54.077
compatible saved representation.

00:01:54.077 --> 00:01:57.090
The output stream writes our card, and the input

00:01:57.090 --> 00:01:58.408
stream reads it back.

00:01:58.408 --> 00:02:01.421
The readObject method returns Object, the common

00:02:01.421 --> 00:02:02.362
reference type.

00:02:02.362 --> 00:02:05.689
The runtime type check, instanceof, tests whether the

00:02:05.689 --> 00:02:08.011
actual non-null value is a ScoreCard.

00:02:08.011 --> 00:02:11.338
The reference cast then gives that reference the more

00:02:11.338 --> 00:02:14.539
specific ScoreCard type so we can call its methods.

00:02:14.539 --> 00:02:16.611
Other types take the else branch.

00:02:16.611 --> 00:02:19.812
Try with resources closes both streams, and finally

00:02:19.812 --> 00:02:20.879
deletes the file.

00:02:20.994 --> 00:02:23.012
Predict all three printed lines.

00:02:23.012 --> 00:02:26.166
Which fields are saved, and what do you expect for

00:02:26.166 --> 00:02:27.869
views on the restored card?

00:02:27.869 --> 00:02:30.518
Decide whether restoring should repeat the

00:02:30.518 --> 00:02:31.275
constructor.

00:02:31.275 --> 00:02:31.969
Pause here.

00:02:37.003 --> 00:02:38.971
Maya and seven are restored.

00:02:38.971 --> 00:02:42.487
Original views is one, but restored views is zero.

00:02:42.487 --> 00:02:46.213
Default deserialization of this ordinary Serializable

00:02:46.213 --> 00:02:49.446
class does not replay its constructor or field

00:02:49.446 --> 00:02:50.360
initializers.

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:53.665
The transient int keeps its default zero value.

00:02:53.665 --> 00:02:57.391
Our type check succeeds, so the cast and getter calls

00:02:57.391 --> 00:02:57.953
proceed.

00:02:58.292 --> 00:03:01.875
Set the constructor’s views value to five and predict

00:03:01.875 --> 00:03:02.957
both view lines.

00:03:02.957 --> 00:03:05.661
Then remove transient and predict again.

00:03:05.661 --> 00:03:09.042
Explain which state belongs in a saved score card.
